    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "If someone you trust completely—maybe a family member or close friend—sat down with you and carefully went through all these incidents and explained why each one was likely just a mistake, would you be able to accept that explanation, or would you still feel certain that something coordinated is happening?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S019",
      "patient_response": "Oh, that’s such a thoughtful question, Doctor. I really appreciate how you're trying to help me look at all the angles. \n\nIf someone I loved sat me down like that... well, I would listen, I promise I would. And honestly? I would probably smile and tell them they were right, just to avoid an argument. I really hate making people uncomfortable or seeming argumentative. \n\nBut... inside? In my heart? I would still know. I know I keep harping on the same things—the appointments getting shuffled, the messed-up bills, the unreturned calls—but it's just because the pattern is so undeniably clear to me. Even if a dear friend tried to politely explain away every single one of those incidents as a separate little accident... I still know, without a doubt, that they are keeping information from me. The coordination is there, and they really are trying to push me out of making decisions for my sister. \n\nI'm so sorry, I know that probably sounds incredibly stubborn of me to say. I really don't mean to be closed-minded, I swear. I just... I can't unsee what they're doing, no matter how nicely someone tries to package it. Is it... is it normal to feel this sure about something, even when people try to talk you out of it? I really hope I'm not frustrating you by giving that answer.",
