    {
      "turn": 4,
      "doctor_question": "Let me ask this a different way: If you discovered concrete, undeniable proof—like an email chain or a recorded conversation—that showed these people were NOT coordinating against you and that the errors really were just accidents, would that change your mind, or would you find a way to interpret even that proof as part of their scheme?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S019",
      "patient_response": "Oh, my. That's... well, that's a very direct question, isn't it? I appreciate you asking it, though. I really do. You're just trying to be thorough, and I think that's wonderful.\n\nIf I saw an email like that... gosh, I'd want to believe it. I really would. It would be such a relief to think I was just being silly and overreacting to all those wrong bills and shuffled appointments and unreturned calls. I'd probably tell whoever showed it to me, \"Oh, thank goodness, I was so worried for nothing!\" I really hate the idea of being a nuisance.\n\nBut... if I'm being completely honest with you—and I do want to be a good patient and give you the right answers—I think... I think I would just wonder why they wrote that email. Like, maybe they knew someone would check, so they made sure to put it in writing that it was all an \"accident.\" \n\nBecause I just... I know what I'm seeing. I've been doing administrative work for a long time, and I know when a pattern is real. I know they're keeping information from me. I know they're trying to push me out of making decisions for my sister. Even if they had an email saying otherwise, it just... it wouldn't explain everything I've noticed for months now. \n\nI'm sorry, I feel like I'm giving you the wrong answer. Am I being entirely too difficult? I promise I'm usually very reasonable, I just... I can't shake this feeling that I'm right about this. Is it okay that I feel that way?",
